From cfeeecbcccdbeefffbbf7ac03fcd98cfb577d954 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Raul Hernandez <me@spaceface.dev>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 17:47:17 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] native_sim: add the cosmocc toolchain

cosmocc is GCC and GNU binutils for Cosmopolitan Libc. With it,
native_sim builds and runs on Linux and macOS (x86-64 and AArch64) and
on Windows (x86-64), and twister runs native_sim tests there.

cmake/toolchain/cosmocc:
- Select cosmocc's GCC and binutils for the host's CPU, 64-bit only,
  with Cosmopolitan's ABI flags.
- Zephyr's code uses Zephyr's minimal C library, with a 16 KiB heap as
  native_sim has with its other C libraries. cosmocc 4.0.2 has no
  freestanding stddef.h or stdarg.h: they are added where GCC looks for
  them.
  Picolibc would need more, so it is not supported.
- Build the native simulator runner with cosmocc's driver, for
  Cosmopolitan Libc, without its preparation of every function for
  Cosmopolitan's call tracer, which costs speed.
- Executables are for the build host only: ELF linked with cosmocc's
  Linux-only libraries on Linux, PE on Windows. cosmocc cannot make
  Mach-O executables that macOS accepts: there, zephyr.exe is a script
  that starts zephyr.exe.dbg through Cosmopolitan's loader.
  COSMOCC_PORTABLE instead builds Actually Portable Executables, for all
  of cosmocc's operating systems. Windows refuses the driver's PE files
  when sections other than .data end the data: they are padded.
- With a cosmocc that has link time optimization, CONFIG_LTO works.
  Cosmopolitan's ABI flags also go to the link, where LTO generates
  code.
- Outside Windows, CMake starts cosmocc's tools through shell launchers.
  These, the compile and link scripts and the macOS loader are kept in
  Zephyr's user cache, per cosmocc: a cosmocc with GCC's freestanding
  headers may be read-only. The scripts are named per kind of
  executable, so that builds of different kinds can share one cosmocc.

Elsewhere:
- FindHostTools: keep the cosmocc variant for native targets.
- LTO of native libraries: a toolchain can declare that it supports it
  (TOOLCHAIN_SUPPORTS_NATIVE_LIBRARY_LTO). The partial link then
  outputs code (-flinker-output=nolto-rel), and optimizes the library as
  a whole program (-fwhole-program): only its NATIVE_SIMULATOR_IF
  interface is visible outside it, and those macros now also mark it
  externally_visible.
- arch/posix: allow other build hosts when cross-compiling for an
  explicit NATIVE_TARGET_HOST.
- natsim_config: let a toolchain choose the runner's compiler driver,
  and how it links executables (NSI_LINKER).
- Pass the native simulator's INSERT linker scripts with -Wl,-T, not -T:
  GCC moves -T options after all others, behind cosmocc's own linker
  script, where GNU ld cannot insert into it. Otherwise, it is the same.
- natsim_linker_script.ld: keep only allocated symbol_to_keep sections.
  An empty one is not allocated (zephyr.elf is a partial link), and
  it drew other unallocated sections amid the program's memory.
- Check init priorities in the executable's symbols, which cosmocc
  writes to zephyr.exe.dbg.
- nsi_errno.c: the errno table is not const, as Cosmopolitan's errno
  values are variables.
- The native simulator's Makefile builds extra sources and localizes
  embedded images given by absolute paths with rules of their own, as
  Windows paths contain a drive letter's colon.
- timer_model.c: fall back to CLOCK_MONOTONIC where CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW
  is not available (Intel macOS under Cosmopolitan).
- twister: build posix platforms with the cosmocc variant when selected,
  run native platforms on macOS and Windows with it, and list it for
  native_sim/native/64.
---
